/**
 * physics/elementInfo.js
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Descriptive chemistry for all 118 elements: a standard nine-way category
 * (used to color the periodic table grid, following the convention used on
 * most printed tables) plus a fuller, factual write-up of each element's
 * discovery context (who/when/why/how) and its real-world properties,
 * chemistry and uses — shown when that element is selected in the Atomic
 * Structure lab.
 *
 * Categories follow the widely-taught classification. A few boundary calls
 * are genuinely debated among chemists (e.g. whether Zn/Cd/Hg count as
 * "transition metals" since their d-subshell is full, or exactly which six
 * or seven elements are "metalloids"); this file makes the same choices
 * most general-chemistry textbooks and periodic table posters do. For the
 * heaviest synthetic elements (roughly Z ≥ 104), only a few atoms have ever
 * been produced, each surviving fractions of a second — their listed
 * category and any chemical behavior is therefore extrapolated from
 * periodic trends and quantum-chemical prediction, not direct observation;
 * the summaries say so explicitly where that matters.
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

export const CATEGORY_LABEL = {
  alkali: "Alkali metal",
  alkaline: "Alkaline earth metal",
  transition: "Transition metal",
  lanthanide: "Lanthanide",
  actinide: "Actinide",
  posttransition: "Post-transition metal",
  metalloid: "Metalloid",
  nonmetal: "Nonmetal",
  halogen: "Halogen",
  noble: "Noble gas",
};

export const CATEGORY_COLOR = {
  alkali: "#ef5b6f",
  alkaline: "#f2a94e",
  transition: "#5b8def",
  lanthanide: "#c084fc",
  actinide: "#a374e8",
  posttransition: "#6bd68a",
  metalloid: "#d9c25c",
  nonmetal: "#4fd8e0",
  halogen: "#35b6c9",
  noble: "#8fa8c8",
};

// [category, summary] keyed by Z, 1..118.
const RAW = {
  1: ["nonmetal", "Cavendish identified hydrogen as a distinct substance while studying flammable gases from metals and acids; Antoine Lavoisier later named it hydrogen, meaning ‘water-former’. Hydrogen is the lightest element and the most abundant element in the universe. Its atom has one proton and one electron. It commonly forms +1 oxidation states, but hydrides can contain H−. H2 is a colorless, odorless, highly flammable diatomic gas. It forms water with oxygen and is important in acids, organic compounds, ammonia manufacture, petroleum processing and stars. Isotopes include protium, deuterium and radioactive tritium. Its low density and high energy per unit mass make it interesting as an energy carrier, although storage and production are major engineering issues."],
  2: ["noble", "Helium was first detected spectroscopically in sunlight during a solar eclipse; it was named after Helios, the Greek Sun god. It was isolated on Earth later by William Ramsay in 1895. Helium is a chemically inert noble gas with a closed 1s2 electron shell. It is extremely low-density and has an exceptionally low boiling point, making liquid helium essential for cooling superconducting magnets and some scientific instruments. It is nonflammable and does not readily form ordinary chemical compounds. Helium is used in cryogenics, leak detection, gas chromatography, welding atmospheres, balloons and some medical/scientific equipment. Most terrestrial helium is trapped in natural-gas deposits and is produced by radioactive decay of uranium and thorium in rocks."],
  3: ["alkali", "Arfwedson discovered lithium as a new element while analyzing the mineral petalite. Berzelius and others helped establish its name and chemistry. Lithium is the lightest metal and the lightest alkali metal. It is soft, silvery and highly reactive, especially with water, producing lithium hydroxide and hydrogen. It has one valence electron and commonly forms Li+. Lithium compounds are used in rechargeable batteries, glass and ceramics, lubricating greases and some specialized industrial applications. Lithium metal is also important in research into fusion materials. It has two stable isotopes, Li-6 and Li-7. Because alkali metals are reactive, lithium is not found as a free metal in nature; it occurs in minerals and brines."],
  4: ["alkaline", "Vauquelin identified beryllium in beryl and emerald. Friedrich Wöhler and Antoine Bussy independently isolated the metal in 1828. Beryllium is a light, stiff alkaline-earth metal with high thermal conductivity and a high melting point. It commonly forms Be2+. Its compounds and metal are useful where low mass, rigidity and dimensional stability are valuable. Beryllium is used in some aerospace and precision components, X-ray equipment and specialized copper-beryllium alloys. Beryllium compounds and dust are hazardous if inhaled, so industrial handling requires strict controls. Natural beryllium is mostly Be-9. Its oxide, BeO, has useful thermal and electrical properties."],
  5: ["metalloid", "Gay-Lussac and Thénard produced impure boron in 1808, while Davy independently obtained boron around the same time. Boron is a hard metalloid with complex structures and chemistry. It has three valence electrons and commonly forms covalent compounds rather than a simple B3+ ion. Boron compounds are important in borosilicate glass, detergents, ceramics, semiconductor technology and neutron-absorbing materials. Boron carbide is extremely hard. Boron is also a micronutrient required by plants, though excessive exposure can be harmful. Natural boron consists mainly of B-11 and B-10; B-10 has a high neutron-capture cross section and is used in nuclear applications."],
  6: ["nonmetal", "Charcoal, soot and graphite were known in antiquity. Diamond was also known, but the relationship among these forms was not established until modern chemistry; Lavoisier recognized carbon as an element in the 18th century. Carbon is exceptionally versatile because it forms four covalent bonds and can make chains, rings and three-dimensional networks. Major allotropes include diamond, graphite, graphene and fullerenes, each with very different properties. Carbon is the backbone of organic chemistry and is present in living organisms, fuels, plastics and biomolecules. Carbon dioxide is central to the carbon cycle and climate system. Radioactive carbon-14 is used in radiocarbon dating. Carbon is also essential in steelmaking, electrodes, filtration and many industrial materials."],
  7: ["nonmetal", "Rutherford identified a component of air that did not support combustion or respiration and called it ‘noxious air’; Lavoisier and later chemists established its elemental status. Nitrogen is a colorless, largely unreactive diatomic gas making up about 78% of Earth’s atmosphere. Molecular N2 is unusually stable because of its strong triple bond. Nitrogen is essential to proteins, DNA and fertilizers. Industrial nitrogen is obtained mainly by separating air. The Haber-Bosch process converts N2 and H2 into ammonia, which underpins modern fertilizer production. Nitrogen also forms many oxidation states from −3 to +5. Liquid nitrogen is widely used as a cryogenic coolant. Nitrogen oxides contribute to atmospheric chemistry and air pollution."],
  8: ["nonmetal", "Priestley produced oxygen by heating mercury oxide and recognized that it strongly supported combustion. Scheele produced it independently earlier, but his work was published later. Lavoisier established its role in combustion and named it oxygen. Oxygen is a highly reactive, colorless diatomic gas that supports combustion and aerobic respiration. It commonly has oxidation state −2, although peroxides and superoxides show other behavior. O2 is essential for most complex life and is produced biologically by photosynthesis. Industrial oxygen is separated from air and used in steelmaking, chemical production, wastewater treatment and medicine. Ozone, O3, is an allotrope with very different atmospheric behavior: stratospheric ozone absorbs ultraviolet radiation while ground-level ozone is an air pollutant."],
  9: ["halogen", "Moissan isolated fluorine by electrolyzing suitable fluoride compounds at low temperatures, solving a notoriously difficult problem because fluorine reacts with many materials. Fluorine is the most electronegative and most reactive element. F2 is a pale yellow, poisonous and highly reactive diatomic gas. Fluorine almost always has oxidation state −1 in compounds. Fluoride forms strong bonds with many elements. Fluorine chemistry is important in fluoropolymers, pharmaceuticals, refrigerants and specialized materials. Fluoride ions are also used in dental products because they can strengthen tooth enamel, but excessive intake can be harmful. Elemental fluorine itself is extremely hazardous and is handled only in specialized systems."],
  10: ["noble", "They discovered neon while separating liquefied air and examining the spectra of the remaining gases. Neon is a noble gas with a filled shell and very low chemical reactivity. It is colorless under normal conditions but produces a characteristic red-orange glow in electrical discharge tubes. This property made neon lighting famous. Neon is also used in indicators, specialized lasers and cryogenic research. It has three stable isotopes. Like other noble gases, it is monoatomic rather than diatomic. Neon is present in Earth’s atmosphere only in trace amounts and is obtained commercially mainly from air-separation processes."],
  11: ["alkali", "Davy isolated sodium by electrolysis of molten sodium hydroxide. Sodium is a soft, silvery alkali metal with one valence electron and a strong tendency to form Na+. It reacts vigorously with water and oxygen, so elemental sodium must be stored away from moisture and air. Sodium compounds are extremely common: sodium chloride is ordinary salt, while sodium hydroxide and sodium carbonate are major industrial chemicals. Sodium ions are essential for nerve signaling, fluid balance and many cellular processes. Sodium vapor lamps historically used its characteristic yellow emission. Natural sodium is overwhelmingly Na-23."],
  12: ["alkaline", "Black recognized magnesia as distinct from calcium compounds; Davy isolated magnesium metal by electrochemical methods. Magnesium is a light, silvery alkaline-earth metal that commonly forms Mg2+. It burns with a very bright white light and reacts with acids. Magnesium alloys are valued for low density, while magnesium compounds are used in refractory materials, agriculture, medicines and industrial chemistry. Mg2+ is biologically important: chlorophyll contains magnesium at its center, and magnesium ions participate in many enzymes and cellular reactions. Natural magnesium has three stable isotopes."],
  13: ["posttransition", "Ørsted obtained impure aluminium from aluminium chloride, while Wöhler improved the isolation method. Aluminium is a light, corrosion-resistant metal because it rapidly forms a thin protective oxide layer. It commonly forms Al3+. It is abundant in Earth’s crust, mainly in aluminosilicate minerals and bauxite. Aluminium is widely used in transportation, construction, packaging, electrical applications and alloys. Its oxide, alumina, is very hard and is used in abrasives and ceramics. Recycling aluminium is energetically advantageous compared with producing it from ore."],
  14: ["metalloid", "Berzelius isolated relatively pure silicon by reducing silicon compounds and established it as a distinct element. Silicon is a metalloid with four valence electrons and is central to modern semiconductor technology. Elemental silicon is a crystalline solid and forms an extensive chemistry of silicates and silicon-oxygen networks. Silicon dioxide is a major component of sand, quartz and glass. High-purity silicon is used for integrated circuits and solar cells. Silicon is also present in concrete, ceramics and silicones. Its semiconductor properties can be controlled through doping, allowing precise electrical behavior."],
  15: ["nonmetal", "Brand discovered phosphorus while experimenting with urine and obtaining a substance that glowed in air; the discovery helped launch phosphorus chemistry. Phosphorus exists in several allotropes, especially white, red and black phosphorus. White phosphorus is reactive and toxic, while red phosphorus is much more stable. Phosphorus commonly appears in oxidation states −3, +3 and +5. Phosphate compounds are essential to DNA, RNA, ATP, bones and cell membranes. Phosphate rock is important for fertilizer production. Phosphorus chemistry also matters in detergents, flame retardants, matches and specialty materials."],
  16: ["nonmetal", "Sulfur and its minerals were known in ancient civilizations. Lavoisier demonstrated in the 18th century that sulfur should be regarded as an element rather than a compound. Sulfur is a yellow nonmetal that commonly forms oxidation states −2, +4 and +6. It has several allotropes and forms rings such as S8. Sulfuric acid is one of the world’s most important industrial chemicals and is used in fertilizer manufacture, petroleum refining and chemical processing. Sulfur is also present in amino acids such as cysteine and methionine. Elemental sulfur is recovered largely during petroleum and natural-gas processing to control sulfur dioxide emissions."],
  17: ["halogen", "Scheele produced chlorine from manganese dioxide and hydrochloric acid. Davy demonstrated that it was an element rather than an oxygen compound and named it chlorine. Chlorine is a greenish-yellow, reactive diatomic gas. It commonly forms Cl− and many chlorine compounds. Chlorine chemistry is essential for water disinfection and the manufacture of PVC and many industrial chemicals. Chloride ions are also biologically important for fluid balance and stomach acid. Chlorine gas itself is toxic and irritating, so industrial systems use strict containment. Hypochlorite compounds are common disinfectants."],
  18: ["noble", "They identified argon as a previously unknown component of air after noticing that atmospheric nitrogen was slightly denser than nitrogen made chemically. Argon is a colorless, monoatomic noble gas with very low chemical reactivity. It is the third-most abundant gas in Earth’s dry atmosphere. Argon is used as an inert shielding gas in welding and metal processing, where it prevents unwanted reactions with atmospheric oxygen and nitrogen. It is also used in some lamps, insulating windows and scientific instruments. Argon has three naturally occurring stable isotopes."],
  19: ["alkali", "Davy isolated potassium by electrolysis of molten potassium hydroxide. Potassium is a soft, reactive alkali metal that forms K+. It reacts vigorously with water and oxygen. Potassium ions are crucial to nerve impulses, muscle function and cellular osmotic balance. Potassium compounds are important fertilizers because plants require substantial potassium. Potassium metal is sometimes used in specialized chemical and heat-transfer applications. Natural potassium includes radioactive K-40, which contributes to natural background radiation and is useful in geochronology."],
  20: ["alkaline", "Davy isolated calcium by electrolysis of a mixture containing lime and mercuric oxide. Calcium commonly forms Ca2+ and is abundant in minerals such as limestone, gypsum and apatite. It is the main mineral component of bones and teeth and participates in muscle contraction, signaling and blood clotting. Calcium metal is reactive, while calcium compounds are widespread in construction, agriculture and industry. Calcium carbonate is used in cement, paper and fillers; calcium oxide is quicklime; calcium hydroxide is slaked lime."],
  21: ["transition", "Nilson discovered scandium while analyzing minerals containing rare-earth elements; its existence had been predicted by Mendeleev as ‘eka-boron’. Scandium is a silvery transition metal commonly forming Sc3+. It is relatively rare and is usually obtained as a byproduct of other mineral processing. Small amounts of scandium improve aluminium alloys, producing strong lightweight materials useful in specialized aerospace and sporting applications. Scandium compounds also have specialized uses in lighting and research. Its chemistry resembles that of some lanthanides and aluminium."],
  22: ["transition", "Gregor identified titanium in a mineral from Cornwall; Martin Heinrich Klaproth later named it titanium. Titanium is strong, relatively light, corrosion-resistant and able to maintain strength at elevated temperatures. It commonly forms Ti4+. Titanium dioxide is a bright white pigment used in paints and other materials. Titanium metal and alloys are important in aerospace, chemical equipment and some biomedical applications because of strength, corrosion resistance and biocompatibility. The element forms a stable oxide surface that protects the metal."],
  23: ["transition", "Del Río identified vanadium in lead ore from Mexico. The element was initially disputed, but Nils Sefström rediscovered it in 1830 and the name vanadium became established. Vanadium has several oxidation states, especially +2, +3, +4 and +5, giving its compounds varied colors. It is used mainly as an alloying element, particularly in steels where it can improve strength and wear resistance. Vanadium compounds have catalytic applications. Vanadium redox-flow batteries are studied and used for large-scale energy storage because the same element can exist in multiple oxidation states."],
  24: ["transition", "Vauquelin isolated chromium from crocoite ore; its colorful compounds led to the name from the Greek word for color. Chromium is a hard transition metal with oxidation states commonly +2, +3 and +6. Chromium metal provides corrosion resistance and a bright finish, making it important in stainless steel and plating. Chromium(III) compounds and chromium(VI) compounds differ greatly in toxicity and chemistry; hexavalent chromium compounds are hazardous carcinogens. Chromium’s ability to form colorful compounds is a classic feature of transition-metal chemistry."],
  25: ["transition", "Gahn isolated manganese metal by reducing manganese dioxide. Manganese has multiple oxidation states and is especially important in steelmaking. Manganese dioxide is used in some batteries and as an oxidizing/catalytic material. Manganese is also an essential trace element in biology, participating in enzymes and antioxidant systems. Manganese steels can be exceptionally wear-resistant. Its compounds range from pale to intensely colored depending on oxidation state and coordination."],
  26: ["transition", "Iron was known to ancient civilizations, including as meteoritic metal. Large-scale production expanded with iron smelting and later steelmaking; no single modern discoverer exists. Iron is one of the most important structural metals and is central to civilization. It commonly forms Fe2+ and Fe3+ and exhibits magnetic behavior. Steel is mainly iron with controlled amounts of carbon and other elements. Iron is also biologically essential: hemoglobin uses iron to bind oxygen, and many enzymes depend on iron. Rusting is an electrochemical oxidation process involving oxygen and water. Earth’s core is dominated by iron and nickel."],
  27: ["transition", "Brandt demonstrated that cobalt was a distinct metal while investigating ores that had been associated with misleading alchemical explanations. Cobalt is a hard transition metal with useful magnetic, catalytic and high-temperature properties. It commonly forms Co2+ and Co3+. Cobalt compounds can produce strong blue pigments, while cobalt-containing alloys can retain strength at high temperatures. Cobalt is also important in some rechargeable battery chemistries and industrial catalysts. Biologically, cobalt is the central metal in vitamin B12, though elemental cobalt and many compounds require controlled exposure."],
  28: ["transition", "Cronstedt studied a mineral that looked like copper ore but yielded a different metal, leading to nickel’s identification. Nickel is a corrosion-resistant transition metal commonly forming Ni2+. It is important in stainless steels, superalloys, plating, catalysts and some rechargeable batteries. Nickel has useful magnetic properties and can tolerate high temperatures in alloys. Nickel compounds and prolonged occupational exposure to some nickel substances can pose health hazards, so industrial exposure is controlled."],
  29: ["transition", "Copper was used in native form and later extracted from ores in ancient civilizations. The discovery is therefore prehistoric rather than attributable to one person. Copper is an excellent electrical and thermal conductor and is easily shaped. It commonly forms Cu+ and Cu2+. Copper wiring, motors, electronics, plumbing and heat exchangers are major applications. Copper alloys include bronze and brass. Copper ions are essential trace nutrients, while high concentrations can be toxic to organisms. Copper’s characteristic reddish color distinguishes it from most common structural metals."],
  30: ["transition", "Zinc was used in brass in ancient times. Independent isolation history is complex because zinc was produced in Asia before becoming well documented in Europe. Zinc is a bluish-silver metal commonly forming Zn2+. It resists corrosion because it can form protective surface layers and is widely used to galvanize iron and steel. Zinc is also important in brass, die-casting alloys and batteries. Biologically, zinc is an essential trace element in many enzymes and proteins. Zinc oxide is used in materials, coatings and some personal-care products."],
  31: ["posttransition", "He discovered gallium spectroscopically and isolated it soon afterward; its properties closely matched Mendeleev’s predicted ‘eka-aluminium’. Gallium is a soft metal with an unusually low melting point near room temperature but a high boiling point. It commonly forms Ga3+. Gallium compounds such as gallium arsenide and gallium nitride are important semiconductor materials for high-speed electronics, LEDs and power electronics. Gallium metal can wet or embrittle some materials, so it requires appropriate containment. It is present only in trace amounts in many ores and is often recovered during processing of other metals."],
  32: ["metalloid", "Winkler discovered germanium in the mineral argyrodite, confirming Mendeleev’s prediction of an ‘eka-silicon’. Germanium is a brittle metalloid and semiconductor. It commonly forms +2 and +4 oxidation states. Germanium was historically important in early semiconductor devices and remains useful in fiber-optic systems, infrared optics and specialized electronics. Germanium dioxide is used in some optical materials. It is relatively rare in Earth’s crust and is often recovered as a byproduct of zinc processing."],
  33: ["metalloid", "Arsenic compounds were known much earlier, but Albertus Magnus is traditionally credited with obtaining elemental arsenic in medieval Europe. Arsenic is a metalloid with several allotropes and oxidation states. Many arsenic compounds are highly toxic, and inorganic arsenic exposure is a major environmental and public-health concern. Historically, arsenic compounds were used in pigments and pesticides; modern controlled uses include specialized semiconductors such as gallium arsenide and some materials science applications. Arsenic chemistry is strongly dependent on oxidation state and bonding environment."],
  34: ["nonmetal", "They identified selenium while investigating residues from sulfuric-acid production and recognized it as a new element. Selenium is a nonmetal with several allotropes and useful photoconductive properties. It commonly occurs in oxidation states −2, +4 and +6. Selenium is an essential trace nutrient in small quantities because it is incorporated into certain enzymes, but excessive exposure can be harmful. Selenium compounds have applications in glassmaking, electronics, pigments and chemical processing. Selenium photoconductivity historically supported photocopiers and related devices."],
  35: ["halogen", "Balard discovered bromine in brine residues and recognized its characteristic reddish-brown liquid and reactive chemistry. Bromine is one of only two elements that are liquids near room temperature. It is a reddish-brown, volatile diatomic halogen with strong oxidizing behavior. Bromide ions occur naturally in seawater and brines. Bromine compounds have been used in flame retardants, photographic chemistry, pharmaceuticals and water treatment. Some brominated compounds are environmentally persistent or hazardous, so many applications have been restricted or replaced."],
  36: ["noble", "They found krypton while separating liquid air after removing more abundant gases. Krypton is a colorless noble gas with low chemical reactivity. It emits characteristic light in electrical discharges and has been used in specialized lamps, lasers and photographic equipment. Krypton has several stable isotopes and a number of radioactive isotopes. Its atmospheric concentration is very low, and it is recovered from air-separation plants."],
  37: ["alkali", "They identified rubidium spectroscopically through two distinctive red spectral lines while examining mineral residues. Rubidium is a soft alkali metal that readily forms Rb+. It is highly reactive and is not found freely in nature. Rubidium compounds have research and specialized industrial uses. Radioactive Rb-87 is naturally occurring and is used in rubidium-strontium dating of rocks because it decays to Sr-87. Rubidium is also studied in atomic clocks and quantum physics."],
  38: ["alkaline", "Crawford distinguished a new mineral species from barium minerals; Davy later isolated strontium metal in 1808. Strontium is an alkaline-earth metal commonly forming Sr2+. Its compounds can produce a vivid red color in flames, which has led to uses in signal and pyrotechnic compositions. Strontium occurs naturally in minerals and seawater. Radioactive Sr-90 is a significant fission product and must be handled as a radioactive hazard; it is not the same as stable strontium used in ordinary chemistry."],
  39: ["transition", "Gadolin identified yttrium in a mineral from Ytterby, Sweden; later chemists separated the metal and clarified its chemistry. Yttrium is a silvery transition metal commonly forming Y3+. It is important in phosphors, lasers, ceramics and some high-performance materials. Yttrium compounds help produce red phosphors and are used in yttrium-aluminium-garnet lasers. Yttrium is chemically related to the rare-earth elements and occurs with them in minerals."],
  40: ["transition", "Klaproth identified zirconium in zircon; Jöns Jacob Berzelius later obtained the metal. Zirconium is corrosion-resistant and has a high melting point. It commonly forms Zr4+. Zirconium alloys are valuable in nuclear technology because zirconium has a relatively low neutron-capture cross section and good corrosion resistance. Zirconium compounds are also used in ceramics and refractory materials. Hafnium often accompanies zirconium in ores and must be separated carefully for nuclear applications."],
  41: ["transition", "Hatchett identified a new element in columbite; it was initially called columbium. The name niobium was later standardized. Niobium is a corrosion-resistant transition metal commonly forming Nb5+. It is especially valuable in superconducting alloys and in microalloyed steels, where small additions improve strength. Niobium compounds are also used in advanced ceramics and electronics. Niobium-tin and related materials can become superconducting at low temperatures."],
  42: ["transition", "Scheele identified molybdenum as a distinct element in molybdenite; Hjelm subsequently isolated the metal. Molybdenum has a very high melting point and retains strength at elevated temperatures. It commonly forms +4, +5 and +6 compounds. Molybdenum is used in alloy steels, catalysts and high-temperature materials. It is also a trace nutrient in biology and occurs in enzymes involved in nitrogen and sulfur metabolism. Molybdenum disulfide is a useful solid lubricant."],
  43: ["transition", "They identified technetium in molybdenum that had been bombarded with deuterons; it was the first element produced artificially and was later found in tiny natural amounts. Technetium has no stable isotopes and is radioactive. Its most important isotope in medicine is Tc-99m, widely used for diagnostic imaging because it emits useful gamma radiation and has a short half-life. Technetium chemistry is dominated by coordination compounds and multiple oxidation states. Because it is radioactive and scarce in nature, it is normally produced artificially in nuclear facilities."],
  44: ["transition", "Claus isolated ruthenium from platinum-group residues and established it as a distinct element. Ruthenium is a hard platinum-group metal with excellent corrosion resistance and catalytic activity. It forms multiple oxidation states and a wide variety of coordination compounds. It is used in electrical contacts, catalysts, thin films and some chemical processes. Ruthenium compounds are also studied in medicinal chemistry and photochemistry."],
  45: ["transition", "Wollaston discovered rhodium while processing platinum ore and examining colored compounds. Rhodium is a rare, silvery platinum-group metal with exceptional corrosion resistance and catalytic activity. It is widely used in catalytic converters to help reduce harmful exhaust pollutants. Rhodium plating provides a bright, hard surface. Because supply is limited and demand can fluctuate, rhodium can be economically valuable. Its compounds often show multiple oxidation states."],
  46: ["transition", "Wollaston discovered palladium during platinum refining and named it after the asteroid Pallas. Palladium is a platinum-group metal with strong catalytic properties and the unusual ability to absorb significant amounts of hydrogen. It is used in catalytic converters, electronics, hydrogen-related research and chemical catalysis. Palladium commonly forms Pd2+ compounds. It is corrosion-resistant and relatively soft compared with some related metals."],
  47: ["transition", "Silver was used by ancient civilizations as a metal, ornament and currency. Its discovery predates recorded scientific attribution. Silver has the highest electrical and thermal conductivity of any metal under ordinary conditions. It commonly forms Ag+. Silver is used in electronics, mirrors, jewelry, specialized coatings and some antimicrobial applications. Tarnishing occurs mainly through reactions involving sulfur compounds in air. Silver’s scarcity and attractive appearance also made it historically important as money."],
  48: ["transition", "Stromeyer found cadmium as an impurity in zinc carbonate and recognized that it was a new element. Cadmium is a soft, bluish metal commonly forming Cd2+. It was historically used in pigments, coatings and nickel-cadmium batteries. Because cadmium is toxic and can accumulate in organisms, many applications have been restricted. Cadmium compounds require careful handling. CdTe is used in some thin-film solar cells under controlled industrial conditions."],
  49: ["posttransition", "They discovered indium spectroscopically from zinc ores through a distinctive indigo spectral line. Indium is a soft, low-melting metal commonly forming In3+. Its compounds are important in transparent conductive coatings, especially indium tin oxide, used in displays and touch-sensitive electronics. Indium is relatively scarce and is generally recovered as a byproduct of zinc processing. It is valued for its combination of electrical, optical and mechanical properties."],
  50: ["posttransition", "Tin was used early in alloys such as bronze. Its chemistry and metallic nature were known long before modern scientific discovery. Tin commonly forms Sn2+ and Sn4+ and has several allotropes. It is soft, corrosion-resistant and useful as a coating for steel and as a component of solder and alloys. Bronze is primarily copper-tin. Tin has a distinctive phenomenon called tin pest in which low-temperature conditions can transform metallic tin into a brittle allotrope. Tin compounds also have industrial applications."],
  51: ["metalloid", "Antimony compounds and native antimony were known in ancient cultures. There is no single modern discoverer. Antimony is a brittle metalloid commonly showing +3 and +5 oxidation states. It is used in flame-retardant systems, alloys, solders and semiconductor materials. Antimony compounds can be toxic, so exposure is controlled. Antimony has several allotropes and forms compounds with sulfur and metals."],
  52: ["metalloid", "He investigated an unusual ore from Transylvania and concluded that it contained a new element; Klaproth later confirmed it. Tellurium is a brittle metalloid chemically related to selenium. It forms oxidation states −2, +4 and +6 and is used in alloys, thermoelectric materials, semiconductors and some solar technologies. Tellurium is relatively rare and often recovered during copper refining. Certain tellurium compounds have distinctive odors and can cause biological effects at elevated exposure."],
  53: ["halogen", "Courtois discovered iodine while processing seaweed ash for saltpeter production; its violet vapor and chemistry led to its identification as a new element. Iodine is a dark, lustrous halogen that sublimes to a violet vapor. It commonly forms iodide, I−, and higher oxidation-state compounds. Iodine is essential for thyroid hormones, which regulate metabolism and development. Iodized salt helps prevent iodine deficiency. Radioactive iodine isotopes have important medical uses under controlled professional settings. Iodine is less reactive than fluorine, chlorine and bromine."],
  54: ["noble", "They discovered xenon while examining residues from liquefied air. Xenon is a heavy noble gas that is chemically quite inert under ordinary conditions, though compounds are known, especially with fluorine and oxygen under specialized conditions. Xenon produces bright light in electrical discharges and is used in specialized lamps, flash systems, ion propulsion research and scientific instruments. Xenon has several stable isotopes and many radioactive isotopes."],
  55: ["alkali", "They identified cesium from mineral-water samples through two distinctive blue spectral lines. Cesium is an extremely reactive, soft alkali metal with one valence electron. It forms Cs+ and reacts strongly with water. Cesium-133 is the stable isotope used to define the SI second through the frequency of its atomic transition. Cesium compounds have specialized uses in chemistry and electronics. Because cesium metal is highly reactive, it is stored under controlled conditions."],
  56: ["alkaline", "Barium was recognized through its mineral and chemical behavior before Davy isolated metallic barium by electrolysis. Barium commonly forms Ba2+. Soluble barium compounds can be toxic, while insoluble barium sulfate is much less soluble and is used as a radiopaque contrast material under medical supervision. Barium compounds also occur in ceramics, glass and drilling fluids. Barium’s characteristic green flame emission is useful in analytical chemistry and pyrotechnic applications."],
  57: ["lanthanide", "Mosander separated lanthanum from cerium-containing material and identified it as a new rare-earth element. Lanthanum is a soft, silvery lanthanide that commonly forms La3+. It is used in optical glass, catalysts, rechargeable-battery materials and specialized ceramics. Lanthanum compounds have strong ionic character and are often handled as rare-earth materials. The element’s name comes from a Greek term meaning ‘to lie hidden’, reflecting its difficult discovery among rare-earth minerals."],
  58: ["lanthanide", "Cerium was identified in the mineral cerite by Swedish chemists and independently by Klaproth around the same period; the name honored the asteroid Ceres. Cerium is a common lanthanide and commonly forms Ce3+ and Ce4+. Its +4 state makes cerium unusually useful as an oxidizing agent. Cerium oxide is used in glass polishing and catalytic systems. Cerium compounds also occur in fuel additives and specialty materials. Cerium is relatively abundant among rare-earth elements."],
  59: ["lanthanide", "Welsbach separated praseodymium from didymium, which had previously been treated as a single rare-earth substance. Praseodymium commonly forms Pr3+ and can also show +4 chemistry. Its compounds are used in specialty glasses and ceramics, including materials with useful optical properties. Praseodymium is also used in some high-performance magnets and alloys. Its name refers to a ‘green twin’, reflecting its relationship to neodymium and the color of some compounds."],
  60: ["lanthanide", "Welsbach separated neodymium from didymium and established it as a distinct rare-earth element. Neodymium is a silvery lanthanide commonly forming Nd3+. Neodymium-iron-boron magnets are among the strongest permanent magnets commercially available and are important in motors, generators, speakers and many electronic systems. Neodymium compounds also color glass and are used in lasers. It is one of the most economically important rare-earth elements."],
  61: ["lanthanide", "They identified promethium among fission products of uranium and demonstrated that it was a new radioactive element. Promethium has no stable isotopes and is radioactive. Its most stable isotope, Pm-145, has a long half-life compared with many isotopes but is still radioactive. Promethium has limited applications because it is rare and radioactive. It has been studied in luminous devices and nuclear batteries. Its discovery filled a gap in the lanthanide series."],
  62: ["lanthanide", "He identified samarium in a mineral containing rare-earth elements; the name came from samarskite. Samarium commonly forms Sm3+, with some Sm2+ chemistry. Samarium-cobalt magnets are strong and retain magnetic properties at relatively high temperatures. Samarium compounds also have applications in catalysts, glass and neutron-absorbing materials. Samarium-149 is a strong neutron absorber and is important in nuclear reactor physics."],
  63: ["lanthanide", "Demarçay separated europium from samarium-rich material using spectroscopic evidence. Europium is a soft lanthanide with unusually accessible +2 and +3 oxidation states. Europium compounds are important phosphors, especially for red and some blue emissions in display and lighting technologies. Europium is relatively scarce and is recovered from rare-earth ores. Its sharp optical emissions make it useful for scientific studies and security features."],
  64: ["lanthanide", "Marignac identified gadolinium as a new rare-earth component in a sample derived from samarskite. Gadolinium commonly forms Gd3+ and has unusual magnetic behavior. Some gadolinium compounds are used as contrast agents in magnetic-resonance imaging under medical supervision. Gadolinium has a very high thermal-neutron capture cross section, making it useful in nuclear science. It is also used in phosphors, magnets and specialized materials."],
  65: ["lanthanide", "Mosander separated terbium-containing material from yttrium-related rare-earth mixtures. Terbium commonly forms Tb3+ and has useful magnetic and optical properties. Terbium compounds can produce green phosphorescence and are used in phosphors and magnetostrictive materials. Terbium is relatively scarce among rare earths. Its name comes from Ytterby, Sweden, a locality associated with several element discoveries."],
  66: ["lanthanide", "He isolated dysprosium from holmium-related rare-earth material after extensive separation work. Dysprosium is a lanthanide with strong magnetic behavior and commonly forms Dy3+. It is added to some high-performance permanent magnets to improve thermal stability and coercivity. Dysprosium compounds also have specialized uses in lasers and nuclear applications. Its name derives from Greek for ‘hard to get’, reflecting the difficulty of separating it."],
  67: ["lanthanide", "Cleve identified holmium as a new rare-earth component in erbium-derived material. Holmium is a lanthanide commonly forming Ho3+. It has exceptionally strong magnetic properties and is used in specialized magnets and research. Holmium-doped lasers have applications in medicine and materials processing. Holmium also has a high magnetic moment and is studied in quantum and condensed-matter physics."],
  68: ["lanthanide", "Mosander separated erbium-containing fractions from yttrium-related rare-earth material. Erbium commonly forms Er3+ and is particularly important in optical communications. Erbium-doped fiber amplifiers amplify signals in the telecommunications wavelength range without converting them back to electrical form. Erbium compounds can produce pink coloration in glass. The element also appears in lasers and specialty ceramics."],
  69: ["lanthanide", "Cleve identified thulium as a distinct rare-earth component in erbium-related material. Thulium is one of the least abundant naturally occurring lanthanides. It commonly forms Tm3+ and has specialized uses because of its electronic and optical properties. Thulium-doped lasers are used in some medical and industrial systems. Radioactive Tm-170 has been investigated for compact radiation sources."],
  70: ["lanthanide", "Marignac separated ytterbium from erbium-rich material and identified it as a distinct rare earth. Ytterbium commonly forms Yb3+ but can also form Yb2+, making its chemistry somewhat unusual among lanthanides. It is used in specialized lasers, alloys and atomic-clock research. Ytterbium-doped fiber lasers are important in high-power photonics. Its name comes from Ytterby, Sweden."],
  71: ["lanthanide", "Urbain and Welsbach independently separated lutetium from ytterbium-rich material, completing the known lanthanide sequence. Lutetium is a dense, hard lanthanide commonly forming Lu3+. It is among the rarest naturally occurring lanthanides. Lutetium compounds have specialized catalytic and optical uses, while radioactive lutetium isotopes are used in some targeted medical applications under clinical control. Its high density and stable +3 chemistry distinguish it from lighter lanthanides."],
  72: ["transition", "They identified hafnium in zirconium minerals using X-ray spectroscopy, confirming that an element chemically similar to zirconium was present. Hafnium commonly forms Hf4+ and is highly corrosion-resistant. It is important in nuclear control-rod materials because hafnium absorbs neutrons strongly. Hafnium oxide is also useful as a high-k dielectric in advanced electronics. Because hafnium and zirconium have very similar chemistry, separating them is difficult."],
  73: ["transition", "Ekeberg identified tantalum in minerals and named it after Tantalus from Greek mythology because of the difficulty separating it from acids. Tantalum is highly corrosion-resistant and has a high melting point. It commonly forms Ta5+. Tantalum is widely used in capacitors and in chemical equipment. Its oxide forms a stable dielectric layer, enabling compact capacitors. Tantalum is also used in high-temperature alloys and some biomedical materials."],
  74: ["transition", "The Elhuyar brothers isolated tungsten from wolframite-derived material, following earlier recognition of tungsten compounds. Tungsten has the highest melting point of all elements except carbon is often discussed separately because carbon is not a conventional melting comparison; among metals tungsten is exceptionally high. It is dense, hard and heat-resistant. Tungsten is used in high-temperature applications, hard materials such as tungsten carbide, electrical contacts and specialized alloys. It commonly shows +6 chemistry."],
  75: ["transition", "They discovered rhenium spectroscopically in minerals and completed the search for an element predicted by Mendeleev below manganese. Rhenium is extremely dense, rare and resistant to high temperatures. It commonly forms +7 and lower oxidation states. Rhenium is used in superalloys for turbine engines and in catalysts, especially for petroleum-related processes. It is one of the rarest elements in Earth’s crust and is often recovered as a byproduct of molybdenum processing."],
  76: ["transition", "Tennant identified osmium and iridium in platinum residues through their distinctive compounds. Osmium is the densest naturally occurring element commonly cited under standard conditions. It is a hard platinum-group metal with high corrosion resistance. Osmium tetroxide is a powerful oxidant and is highly toxic, so it requires specialized handling. Osmium metal and alloys have niche applications in wear-resistant contacts and scientific instruments."],
  77: ["transition", "Tennant discovered iridium along with osmium while analyzing insoluble platinum residues. Iridium is extremely corrosion-resistant and dense, with a high melting point. It commonly forms +3 and +4 states but has richer coordination chemistry. Iridium alloys are used in high-temperature and chemically demanding environments. Iridium compounds are important in catalysis and photochemistry. The element’s name comes from Iris, associated with the rainbow, reflecting the colors of its compounds."],
  78: ["transition", "Ulloa described platinum from South America to European scientific audiences; indigenous peoples had worked platinum-containing materials earlier. Platinum is a dense, malleable, corrosion-resistant platinum-group metal. It is an excellent catalyst and is widely used in catalytic converters and chemical processes. Platinum compounds and surfaces have important roles in medicine and electronics. Platinum is relatively rare and valuable. It commonly forms Pt2+ and Pt4+ coordination compounds."],
  79: ["transition", "Gold occurs naturally as metallic nuggets and was worked by prehistoric peoples, so there is no single discoverer. Gold is a dense, soft, highly malleable metal that resists oxidation and corrosion. It commonly forms Au+ and Au3+. Its chemical inertness and conductivity make it valuable in jewelry, electronics and specialized coatings. Gold also has unusual nanochemistry, with gold nanoparticles showing properties distinct from bulk metal. It has been used as money and a store of value for thousands of years."],
  80: ["transition", "Mercury was known to ancient civilizations and occurs in cinnabar. Its unusual liquid metallic state made it historically distinctive. Mercury is a dense metal that is liquid at ordinary room temperature. It forms Hg+ and Hg2+ compounds and readily forms amalgams with many metals. Mercury was historically used in thermometers, switches, lamps and industrial processes, but many uses have been restricted because mercury and several of its compounds are toxic. Environmental mercury can bioaccumulate, especially in aquatic food webs."],
  81: ["posttransition", "Crookes identified thallium through a distinctive green spectral line while analyzing residues from sulfuric-acid production. Thallium is a soft, heavy metal commonly forming Tl+ and Tl3+. Many thallium compounds are highly toxic because Tl+ can interfere with biological processes by mimicking potassium. Thallium had historical uses in pesticides and specialized electronics, but toxicity has sharply limited applications. Some thallium compounds are used in controlled scientific and industrial contexts."],
  82: ["posttransition", "Lead was smelted and used by ancient civilizations, including in pipes, weights and pigments. Lead is a dense, soft metal commonly forming Pb2+ and Pb4+. It has a low melting point and good corrosion resistance in some environments. Lead-acid batteries remain a major use because they are rechargeable and inexpensive. Lead is toxic, especially to developing nervous systems, and exposure is carefully regulated. Lead was historically used in paints, gasoline additives and plumbing, but many such uses have been phased out."],
  83: ["posttransition", "Geoffroy demonstrated that bismuth was distinct from lead and other metals. Bismuth is a dense, brittle, relatively low-melting metal with a distinctive crystalline appearance. It commonly forms Bi3+. Bismuth compounds are used in pharmaceuticals, cosmetics, pigments and specialized materials. Compared with many neighboring heavy metals, bismuth compounds are generally less toxic, though specific compounds and exposures still require care. Bismuth has an exceptionally long-lived radioactive isotope, Bi-209, which was once treated as stable."],
  84: ["metalloid", "They discovered polonium in uranium ore by detecting radioactivity stronger than could be explained by uranium alone; they named it after Marie Curie’s homeland, Poland. Polonium is a highly radioactive element with no stable isotopes. It is chemically related to tellurium and commonly forms +2 and +4 states. Polonium’s intense radioactivity makes it useful only in specialized applications, such as certain static-elimination devices historically. It is extremely hazardous because radioactive material can damage living tissue. It occurs naturally in tiny quantities in uranium decay chains."],
  85: ["halogen", "They produced astatine by bombarding bismuth with alpha particles and identified the new radioactive element. Astatine is an extremely rare, radioactive halogen. All its isotopes are radioactive and short-lived compared with geological timescales. Its chemistry resembles iodine but is influenced by strong relativistic effects and metallic character. Because only tiny quantities exist at any time, many physical properties are difficult to measure directly. Astatine is studied mainly in nuclear and radiochemistry, including research on targeted alpha-emitting medical isotopes."],
  86: ["noble", "Dorn observed the radioactive gas released from radium and initially called it a radium emanation. Radon is a radioactive noble gas produced naturally in uranium and thorium decay chains. It can accumulate indoors when soil gases enter buildings. Long-term exposure to elevated radon is associated with increased lung-cancer risk, making radon testing and mitigation important in affected areas. Because radon is chemically inert, its primary hazard is its radioactive decay products rather than ordinary chemical toxicity."],
  87: ["alkali", "Perey identified francium as a decay product of actinium and recognized it as the missing alkali element below cesium. Francium is an extremely rare, highly radioactive alkali metal. All its isotopes are radioactive; the longest-lived naturally occurring isotope is Fr-223. Because only tiny amounts exist naturally, many properties are inferred from periodic trends and limited experiments. Francium is expected to be extremely reactive and to form Fr+. It has essentially no commercial use and is studied mainly in nuclear physics and atomic science."],
  88: ["alkaline", "They separated radium from uranium ore residues by chemical processing and detected its intense radioactivity. Radium is a radioactive alkaline-earth element that commonly forms Ra2+. Its decay produces radon and a chain of radioactive daughter products. Radium compounds were historically used in luminous paints and early medical treatments, but these uses caused serious radiation exposure and were abandoned or tightly controlled. Today radium is mainly important in radiochemistry and research."],
  89: ["actinide", "Debierne identified actinium in uranium-ore residues and recognized it as a new radioactive element. Actinium is a radioactive actinide that commonly forms Ac3+. It occurs naturally in trace quantities in uranium decay chains. Its radioactivity and scarcity make it mainly a research element. Ac-225 is of major interest in targeted alpha therapy research because its decay releases alpha particles that can damage nearby targeted cells; medical use requires specialized production and professional handling."],
  90: ["actinide", "Esmark found thorium minerals, and Berzelius subsequently isolated thorium and named it after the Norse god Thor. Thorium is a naturally radioactive actinide, commonly forming Th4+. Thorium-232 is fertile rather than directly fissile with thermal neutrons; it can absorb neutrons and ultimately be converted into uranium-233 in nuclear fuel cycles. Thorium compounds occur in minerals such as monazite. Thorium has also been used in specialized alloys and historically in gas mantles."],
  91: ["actinide", "Fajans and Göhring identified a short-lived isotope, while later work established and isolated longer-lived protactinium material. Protactinium is a radioactive actinide, commonly showing +5 chemistry. It is extremely rare and occurs in uranium decay chains. Because it is radioactive and scarce, it has little practical use outside nuclear and radiochemical research. Its name means ‘parent of actinium’, reflecting its decay relationship to actinium."],
  92: ["actinide", "Klaproth identified uranium in pitchblende and named it after the recently discovered planet Uranus; metallic uranium was isolated later by Eugène-Melchior Péligot. Uranium is a dense radioactive actinide with multiple oxidation states, especially +4 and +6. U-235 is fissile and is important in nuclear reactors and weapons; U-238 is much more abundant and is fertile. Uranium compounds and ores require radiological and chemical controls. Uranium has played a major role in nuclear physics, energy and geopolitics. It also occurs naturally in trace amounts in rocks and water."],
  93: ["actinide", "They produced neptunium by bombarding uranium with neutrons and identified the first transuranium element. Neptunium is radioactive and belongs to the actinides. Np-237 is the longest-lived isotope and is produced in nuclear fuel cycles. It commonly exhibits +3, +4, +5 and +6 oxidation states, giving it rich aqueous chemistry. Neptunium is primarily important in nuclear science, reactor chemistry and radioactive-waste studies."],
  94: ["actinide", "They produced and identified plutonium by bombarding uranium-derived material with deuterons. Plutonium is a radioactive actinide with several oxidation states and complex metallurgy. Pu-239 is fissile and has been important in nuclear weapons and reactor fuel. Plutonium requires stringent radiological and security controls. Different plutonium allotropes and phases have unusual density and mechanical properties, making its metallurgy technically challenging. Its environmental behavior is important in nuclear-waste science."],
  95: ["actinide", "They produced americium by neutron bombardment of plutonium and identified it as the element analogous to europium in the lanthanide series. Americium is radioactive and commonly forms Am3+, although other oxidation states occur. Am-241 is used in some ionization smoke detectors because it is a practical alpha-particle source. Americium is produced artificially and must be handled under radiation-safety controls. Its chemistry is important in actinide separation and nuclear-waste research."],
  96: ["actinide", "They produced curium by bombarding plutonium with alpha particles and identified the new element. Curium is a radioactive actinide named in honor of Marie and Pierre Curie. It commonly forms Cm3+ and has strong alpha radioactivity. Curium isotopes are used mainly as research and radiation sources. Cm-244 has been used as a power source in specialized radioisotope systems. Curium chemistry helps scientists understand heavy-element bonding and nuclear behavior."],
  97: ["actinide", "They produced berkelium by bombarding americium with alpha particles at Berkeley, California. Berkelium is a synthetic radioactive actinide. It commonly forms Bk3+ and has no stable isotopes. Because it is produced only in tiny quantities, its main role is scientific research. Berkelium has been used as a target material for creating still heavier elements."],
  98: ["actinide", "They produced californium by bombarding curium with alpha particles. Californium is a synthetic radioactive actinide. Cf-252 is a powerful neutron emitter and has specialized uses as a neutron source in research, industrial measurements and some nuclear applications. Because californium is expensive and radioactive, quantities are tightly controlled. It commonly forms Cf3+."],
  99: ["actinide", "Einsteinium was identified in debris from the first thermonuclear test, where intense neutron flux produced heavy nuclei. Einsteinium is a synthetic radioactive actinide named for Albert Einstein. It has no stable isotopes and is produced in extremely small quantities. Es3+ is its common oxidation state. Its primary importance is fundamental research into heavy-element chemistry and nuclear decay."],
  100: ["actinide", "Fermium was identified in the same thermonuclear-test debris as einsteinium and named after Enrico Fermi. Fermium is a synthetic radioactive actinide with no stable isotopes. It is produced in extremely small quantities and is studied primarily through nuclear decay and limited chemical experiments. Fermium commonly exhibits +3 chemistry. Its discovery demonstrated that nuclear reactions could produce elements much heavier than uranium."],
  101: ["actinide", "They produced mendelevium by bombarding einsteinium with alpha particles and detected its characteristic decay. Mendelevium is a synthetic radioactive actinide named for Dmitri Mendeleev. Only tiny amounts can be produced, so its properties are inferred from limited experiments and periodic trends. It commonly forms Md3+, with evidence for Md2+ chemistry under particular conditions. Its main role is fundamental nuclear and chemical research."],
  102: ["actinide", "Nobelium was first reported in Dubna, but the result was disputed and later experiments at Berkeley established the element more securely. It was named after Alfred Nobel. Nobelium is a synthetic radioactive actinide with no stable isotopes. It is produced in tiny quantities and commonly behaves as No2+ in aqueous chemistry, an unusual feature among heavier actinides, with No3+ also possible. Its chemistry is studied to understand relativistic effects and the changing behavior of heavy elements."],
  103: ["actinide", "They produced lawrencium by bombarding californium with boron ions and observed its decay products. Lawrencium is a synthetic radioactive actinide named after Ernest Lawrence. It has no stable isotopes and is produced atom by atom or in very small numbers. Its chemistry is difficult to study, but evidence suggests unusual behavior near the end of the actinide series, including possible preference for a +1 state in some contexts. Its importance is primarily fundamental research."],
  104: ["transition", "Rutherfordium was first reported at Dubna and later independently produced at Berkeley. It was named after Ernest Rutherford after the naming dispute was resolved. Rutherfordium is a synthetic, highly radioactive element expected to resemble zirconium and hafnium as a group-4 metal. It has no stable isotopes and is made in nuclear reactions in tiny quantities. Its chemistry is studied through very rapid experiments because its isotopes decay quickly. The element is mainly important for understanding the transition from actinides to superheavy elements."],
  105: ["transition", "Dubnium was independently reported by researchers at Dubna and Berkeley; the element was eventually named after Dubna, Russia. Dubnium is a synthetic radioactive group-5 element expected to resemble tantalum and niobium. It has no stable isotopes and is produced only in nuclear reactions. Experiments suggest that its chemistry is broadly consistent with a high oxidation state such as +5, but measurements are limited by its short-lived isotopes."],
  106: ["transition", "Teams at Dubna and Berkeley independently reported seaborgium around the same period; it was later named for Glenn T. Seaborg. Seaborgium is a synthetic radioactive group-6 element expected to resemble tungsten and molybdenum. Its chemistry is difficult to study because only small numbers of atoms are produced and they decay rapidly. Experiments support formation of high oxidation-state compounds, helping confirm periodic trends in the superheavy region."],
  107: ["transition", "The element was produced at the Gesellschaft für Schwerionenforschung in Germany by fusing heavy nuclei. Bohrium is a synthetic radioactive group-7 element expected to resemble rhenium. Its isotopes are very short-lived and are created through nuclear fusion reactions. Only a few atoms can be produced in many experiments, so most chemical properties are predicted from periodic trends. It is important in studies of superheavy nuclei and nuclear stability."],
  108: ["transition", "Hassium was produced at GSI by fusion reactions involving iron ions and a heavy target. Hassium is a synthetic radioactive group-8 element expected to resemble osmium. It has no stable isotopes and is produced atom by atom. Chemical studies suggest behavior consistent with a heavy transition metal, including volatile oxide chemistry. Hassium research helps test how periodic trends change under strong relativistic effects."],
  109: ["transition", "Meitnerium was produced by bombarding bismuth with iron nuclei. It was named after physicist Lise Meitner. Meitnerium is a synthetic radioactive element in group 9. Its isotopes are extremely short-lived and only tiny numbers of atoms have been observed. Its chemistry is therefore largely predicted from neighboring elements such as iridium and cobalt. Its main importance is nuclear physics and the study of the limits of the periodic table."],
  110: ["transition", "Darmstadtium was produced at GSI in Darmstadt, Germany, by fusing nickel ions with a lead target. Darmstadtium is a synthetic group-10 element expected to resemble nickel, palladium and platinum. It has no stable isotopes and decays rapidly. Chemical experiments are extremely difficult because production rates are tiny. The element is studied to test predictions about superheavy transition-metal behavior."],
  111: ["transition", "Roentgenium was produced by fusing nickel with bismuth and was named after Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen. Roentgenium is a synthetic group-11 element expected to be chemically related to copper, silver and gold. Its isotopes are highly radioactive and short-lived. Relativistic effects may alter its chemistry significantly compared with lighter group-11 metals. Its known properties are mainly nuclear rather than bulk-material properties."],
  112: ["transition", "Copernicium was produced by fusing zinc ions with lead and was named after Nicolaus Copernicus. Copernicium is a synthetic radioactive group-12 element. It is expected to have unusual volatility and strong relativistic effects, possibly making it less chemically metallic than ordinary group-12 elements. It has no stable isotopes and is studied atom by atom. Its name honors Copernicus."],
  113: ["posttransition", "Researchers at RIKEN produced nihonium by repeatedly bombarding bismuth with zinc and observing decay chains. It was named after Japan, ‘Nihon’. Nihonium is a synthetic radioactive group-13 element. It has no stable isotopes and is produced only in tiny quantities. Its chemistry is expected to resemble thallium but may be strongly affected by relativistic effects. Its discovery demonstrated the ability to build heavier nuclei through fusion and identify them from characteristic decay chains."],
  114: ["posttransition", "Flerovium was produced by fusing calcium ions with plutonium and was named for the Flerov Laboratory of Nuclear Reactions and Georgy Flerov. Flerovium is a synthetic superheavy element. Its predicted chemistry is unusual because relativistic effects strongly influence its outer electrons. It is expected to be relatively volatile for a heavy metal and may show oxidation states different from simple group trends. Its isotopes are radioactive and short-lived."],
  115: ["posttransition", "Moscovium was produced by bombarding americium with calcium ions. The name honors the Moscow region. Moscovium is a synthetic radioactive group-15 element. Its chemistry is difficult to measure directly, but theoretical models predict strong relativistic effects and possible stabilization of lower oxidation states. It is mainly studied through nuclear decay chains and the search for greater nuclear stability."],
  116: ["posttransition", "Livermorium was produced by fusing calcium with curium and was named after the Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory. Livermorium is a synthetic radioactive group-16 element expected to resemble polonium, but with stronger relativistic effects. It has no stable isotopes and is produced in very small quantities. Its discovery contributes to understanding the predicted ‘island of stability’ and the changing chemistry of superheavy nuclei."],
  117: ["halogen", "Tennessine was produced by bombarding berkelium with calcium ions. The name honors Tennessee, a major center of nuclear research. Tennessine is a synthetic radioactive group-17 element. Unlike lighter halogens, its chemistry may be strongly altered by relativistic effects and its short half-lives make experiments difficult. It is expected to have some halogen-like behavior but may be less reactive and more metallic than astatine. Its discovery extended the periodic table into the seventh period."],
  118: ["noble", "Oganesson was produced by fusing calcium with californium and was named after physicist Yuri Oganessian. Oganesson is the heaviest confirmed element. It is a synthetic radioactive group-18 element with no stable isotopes. Although placed among noble gases, relativistic and quantum effects may make its properties very different from helium, neon and argon; models predict unusually strong intermolecular interactions and possibly a condensed state under conditions where lighter noble gases remain gaseous. Its study is almost entirely nuclear and theoretical because individual atoms decay rapidly."],
};

export function getElementInfo(Z) {
  const entry = RAW[Z];
  if (!entry) return { category: "nonmetal", summary: "" };
  return { category: entry[0], summary: entry[1] };
}
